      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Harald Rose",
        "Knut Urban",
        "Max Haider"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Spherical-aberration correction transformed transmission electron microscopy by making substantially higher resolution and more quantitative atomic-scale imaging possible. This is a major instrument invention with broad impact across physics and materials science.",
        "attribution": "Rose supplied foundational correction concepts; Haider and Urban were central to realizing and demonstrating practical corrected microscopes. The proposed trio reflects theory, instrument development, and materials-science validation.",
        "maturity": "The corrected instruments have been established for decades and their capabilities have been repeatedly demonstrated in working microscopes.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The precise division of credit among theoretical proposals, engineering implementation, and early demonstrations requires careful historical review."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B002",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks enable exceptional precision in time and frequency measurement, with consequences for fundamental tests and practical metrology. The invention opened a powerful new measurement regime.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye made leading, independent contributions to the optical lattice clock and its experimental development.",
        "maturity": "Performance has advanced through sustained experimental confirmation, and the technology is now a serious candidate for future time standards.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The balance of credit between the early concepts and later demonstrations should be checked against the detailed historical record."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B045",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Fabiola Gianotti",
        "Peter Jenni",
        "Tejinder Virdee"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The experimental discovery of the Higgs boson confirmed a central prediction of particle physics and was a landmark result in understanding the fundamental constituents of nature.",
        "attribution": "The discovery depended on the ATLAS and CMS collaborations. These names represent major leadership and detector-building contributions, but no small set of individuals can fully express the collective work.",
        "maturity": "The signal was independently observed by two experiments and has since been studied extensively.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Selecting a few laureates from two very large collaborations risks underrepresenting the collective experimental contribution.",
          "The listed names may not fully capture the parallel leadership and instrumental contributions across both experiments."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B005",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Immanuel Bloch",
        "Markus Greiner",
        "Peter Zoller"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical-lattice experiments made controlled quantum simulation of strongly correlated matter possible, creating a new way to investigate many-body physics that is difficult to access in solids.",
        "attribution": "Bloch and Greiner established important experimental capabilities, while Zoller developed key theoretical foundations. The combination of experimental realization and theory is compelling.",
        "maturity": "The field has produced sustained experimental progress and has become a mature research program, though the reach of its most important results continues to develop.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The proposed contribution spans several stages of a broad program, so the most prize-defining discovery would need to be specified."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B020",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Carlton M. Caves",
        "H. Jeff Kimble",
        "Roman Schnabel"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Squeezed light reduces measurement noise below the conventional quantum limit in relevant interferometric measurements. Its use in gravitational-wave detectors demonstrates significant scientific and instrumental value.",
        "attribution": "Caves provided foundational theory, Kimble helped establish experimental methods, and Schnabel contributed to mature squeezed-light sources and applications.",
        "maturity": "The effect is well established and has been implemented in gravitational-wave observatories.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The contributions span theory, laboratory demonstrations, and detector deployment; the boundaries of the prize-worthy invention need historical clarification."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B011",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Juan Ignacio Cirac",
        "Peter Zoller",
        "Rainer Blatt"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Trapped-ion quantum logic joined precise control of individual atoms with a scalable route to quantum computation, while also enabling fundamental studies of quantum information.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller developed influential theoretical foundations, and Blatt led landmark experimental demonstrations of ion-based quantum logic.",
        "maturity": "The core principles have been repeatedly verified, and trapped ions remain among the leading platforms for quantum computation.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field's continuing development makes it difficult to draw a final boundary around the original achievement and subsequent platform advances."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B025",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Charles L. Kane",
        "Eugene J. Mele",
        "Laurens W. Molenkamp"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum spin Hall effect and topological-insulator framework revealed a new class of quantum states with robust boundary behavior, broadening the understanding of phases of matter.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele established the theoretical proposal, and Molenkamp's group provided an influential experimental realization of the quantum spin Hall effect.",
        "maturity": "The foundational predictions and observations have been followed by extensive study across materials and related topological phases.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The proposed discovery groups together a theoretical framework and an experimental effect whose later scope expanded substantially."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Charles L. Bennett",
        "Lyman A. Page Jr.",
        "Jean-Loup Puget"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Precision mapping of cosmic microwave background anisotropies provided strong evidence about the early universe and its large-scale structure, with profound consequences for cosmology.",
        "attribution": "Bennett and Page are closely associated with major anisotropy mapping efforts; Puget represents important contributions to later precision observations. The broader observational history includes multiple missions and large teams.",
        "maturity": "The measurements have been independently refined over decades and underpin the standard cosmological model.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "A previous Nobel Prize recognized foundational CMB measurements, so the distinct advance and its relation to that award need careful definition.",
          "Credit across missions, teams, and successive generations of measurements is difficult to reduce to three people."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
